BOUNDARY CENSUS: over 11 boards, 16 synthetic net name(s) exist in the models and 0 of them reach a global label.
THE BOUNDARY-CENSUS PREDICTION PUBLISHED ABOVE IS REFUTED: it predicted non-zero, on the spec's own mechanism that a declared port makes a net a boundary net. It does not. `buildCircuit` leaves `Circuit.offSheet` empty unconditionally (lib/schgen/ir/build.ts:152, and the field's own comment in lib/schgen/ir/types.ts says only a sheet subset ever has any), so `labelledNets` labels a net only when it is a rail, touches more than one block, or touches a `bus` block. `pt2399-core` is ONE block, so none of its 16 synthetic names is labelled, with or without the fake `clk_o_unused` port.
THE CENSUS IS ZERO, AND WHAT ZERO MEANS: no board in this repository would draw a punctuated net name on a sheet today, so whether KiCad accepts one is NOT a blocker for removing the casing rule - the architecture closes the question and the kicad-cli rows above are a standing answer rather than a precondition. It is NOT a reason to relax a gate: the name still exists in the model, reaches the netlist comparison, and would reach a label the moment a block boundary fell between two pins of one of these nets - which is a layout decision nobody has made yet, not an invariant.

